Early <a href="https://browser.geekbench.com/v6/cpu/19143100">benchmark</a> <a href="https://browser.geekbench.com/v6/cpu/19143028">results</a> for the A20 Pro chip in the iPhone 18 Pro and iPhone 18 Pro Max appeared early this morning in the Geekbench 7 database, offering us a first look at the new models' CPU and graphics performance ahead of the devices reaching customers.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/09/iphone-18-pro.jpg" alt="" width="2027" height="1140" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1063502" /><br/>
Based on these early results – which are unconfirmed – the A20 Pro chip across the Pro models appears to deliver around 21% to 23% faster performance in single-core and 27% to 28% faster performance in multi-core than the A19 Pro chip in the iPhone 17 Pro. It's a pretty impressive jump.<br/>
<br/>
The iPhone 18 Pro result lists a single-core score of 4,719 and a multi-core score of 12,677, while an iPhone 18 Pro Max entry delivers similar results, scoring 4,725 and 12,575, respectively. Both identify a six-core CPU running at approximately 4.93 GHz.<br/>
<br/>
There are similarly formidable gains on the graphics side for the A20 Pro. Based on early <a href="https://browser.geekbench.com/v6/compute/6873610">Metal</a> <a href="https://browser.geekbench.com/v6/compute/6873029">scores</a> in the Geekbench 6 database (also unconfirmed) the full 7-core GPU variant of the chip in the iPhone 18 Pro models is around 38% to 40% faster than the A19 Pro's 6-core GPU.<br/>
<br/>
The graphics results broadly match Apple's claim in its iPhone 18 Pro <a href="https://www.apple.com/newsroom/2026/09/apple-debuts-iphone-18-pro-and-iphone-18-pro-max/">press release</a> and <a href="https://www.apple.com/iphone-18-pro/">product page</a> that the A20 Pro offers up to 20% faster CPU performance compared to the A19 Pro chip in the iPhone 17 Pro models, while the A20 Pro's new seven-core GPU is up to 40% faster than the A19 Pro. <br/>
<br/>
Built using a 2-nanometer process, the chip also features a six-core CPU with integrated Neural Accelerators and 50% more memory bandwidth than its predecessor. Apple also says the new chip packaging and redesigned vapor chamber enable up to 40% better sustained performance than the previous generation. <br/>
<br/>
